.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
The functionality documented below is designed to help with the following operations: 


.IP "\(bu" 2
Enumerating the USB devices currently attached to the system
.IP "\(bu" 2
Choosing a device to operate from your software
.IP "\(bu" 2
Opening and closing the chosen device
.PP
.SH "In a nutshell\&.\&.\&."
.PP
The description below really makes things sound more complicated than they actually are\&. The following sequence of function calls will be suitable for almost all scenarios and does not require you to have such a deep understanding of the resource management issues: 
.PP
.nf
// discover devices
libusb_device **list;
libusb_device *found = NULL;
ssize_t cnt = libusb_get_device_list(NULL, &list);
ssize_t i = 0;
int err = 0;
if (cnt < 0)
    error();

for (i = 0; i < cnt; i++) {
    libusb_device *device = list[i];
    if (is_interesting(device)) {
        found = device;
        break;
    }
}

if (found) {
    libusb_device_handle *handle;

    err = libusb_open(found, &handle);
    if (err)
        error();
    // etc
}

libusb_free_device_list(list, 1);

.fi
.PP

.PP
The two important points:
.IP "\(bu" 2
You asked \fBlibusb_free_device_list()\fP to unreference the devices (2nd parameter)
.IP "\(bu" 2
You opened the device before freeing the list and unreferencing the devices
.PP

.PP
If you ended up with a handle, you can now proceed to perform I/O on the device\&.
.SH "Devices and device handles"
.PP
libusb has a concept of a USB device, represented by the \fBlibusb_device\fP opaque type\&. A device represents a USB device that is currently or was previously connected to the system\&. Using a reference to a device, you can determine certain information about the device (e\&.g\&. you can read the descriptor data)\&.

.PP
The \fBlibusb_get_device_list()\fP function can be used to obtain a list of devices currently connected to the system\&. This is known as device discovery\&. Devices can also be discovered with the hotplug mechanism, whereby a callback function registered with \fBlibusb_hotplug_register_callback()\fP will be called when a device of interest is connected or disconnected\&.

.PP
Just because you have a reference to a device does not mean it is necessarily usable\&. The device may have been unplugged, you may not have permission to operate such device, or another program or driver may be using the device\&.

.PP
When you've found a device that you'd like to operate, you must ask libusb to open the device using the \fBlibusb_open()\fP function\&. Assuming success, libusb then returns you a \fIdevice handle\fP (a \fBlibusb_device_handle\fP pointer)\&. All "real" I/O operations then operate on the handle rather than the original device pointer\&.
.SH "Device discovery and reference counting"
.PP
Device discovery (i\&.e\&. calling \fBlibusb_get_device_list()\fP) returns a freshly-allocated list of devices\&. The list itself must be freed when you are done with it\&. libusb also needs to know when it is OK to free the contents of the list - the devices themselves\&.

.PP
To handle these issues, libusb provides you with two separate items:
.IP "\(bu" 2
A function to free the list itself
.IP "\(bu" 2
A reference counting system for the devices inside
.PP

.PP
New devices presented by the \fBlibusb_get_device_list()\fP function all have a reference count of 1\&. You can increase and decrease reference count using \fBlibusb_ref_device()\fP and \fBlibusb_unref_device()\fP\&. A device is destroyed when its reference count reaches 0\&.

.PP
With the above information in mind, the process of opening a device can be viewed as follows:
.IP "1." 4
Discover devices using \fBlibusb_get_device_list()\fP or \fBlibusb_hotplug_register_callback()\fP\&.
.IP "2." 4
Choose the device that you want to operate, and call \fBlibusb_open()\fP\&.
.IP "3." 4
Unref all devices in the discovered device list\&.
.IP "4." 4
Free the discovered device list\&.
.PP

.PP
The order is important - you must not unreference the device before attempting to open it, because unreferencing it may destroy the device\&.

.PP
For convenience, the \fBlibusb_free_device_list()\fP function includes a parameter to optionally unreference all the devices in the list before freeing the list itself\&. This combines steps 3 and 4 above\&.

.PP
As an implementation detail, \fBlibusb_open()\fP actually adds a reference to the device in question\&. This is because the device remains available through the handle via \fBlibusb_get_device()\fP\&. The reference is deleted during \fBlibusb_close()\fP\&. 
.SH "Typedef Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "typedef struct \fBlibusb_device\fP \fBlibusb_device\fP"

.PP
Structure representing a USB device detected on the system\&. This is an opaque type for which you are only ever provided with a pointer, usually originating from \fBlibusb_get_device_list()\fP or \fBlibusb_hotplug_register_callback()\fP\&.

.PP
Certain operations can be performed on a device, but in order to do any I/O you will have to first obtain a device handle using \fBlibusb_open()\fP\&.

.PP
Devices are reference counted with \fBlibusb_ref_device()\fP and \fBlibusb_unref_device()\fP, and are freed when the reference count reaches 0\&. New devices presented by \fBlibusb_get_device_list()\fP have a reference count of 1, and \fBlibusb_free_device_list()\fP can optionally decrease the reference count on all devices in the list\&. \fBlibusb_open()\fP adds another reference which is later destroyed by \fBlibusb_close()\fP\&. 
.SS "typedef struct \fBlibusb_device_handle\fP \fBlibusb_device_handle\fP"

.PP
Structure representing a handle on a USB device\&. This is an opaque type for which you are only ever provided with a pointer, usually originating from \fBlibusb_open()\fP\&.

.PP
A device handle is used to perform I/O and other operations\&. When finished with a device handle, you should call \fBlibusb_close()\fP\&. 
