.SS "int libusb_wrap_sys_device (\fBlibusb_context\fP * ctx, intptr_t sys_dev, \fBlibusb_device_handle\fP ** dev_handle)"

.PP
Wrap a platform-specific system device handle and obtain a libusb device handle for the underlying device\&. The handle allows you to use libusb to perform I/O on the device in question\&.

.PP
Call libusb_init_context with the LIBUSB_OPTION_NO_DEVICE_DISCOVERY option if you want to skip enumeration of USB devices\&. In particular, this might be needed on Android if you don't have authority to access USB devices in general\&. Setting this option with libusb_set_option is deprecated\&.

.PP
On Linux, the system device handle must be a valid file descriptor opened on the device node\&.

.PP
The system device handle must remain open until \fBlibusb_close()\fP is called\&. The system device handle will not be closed by \fBlibusb_close()\fP\&.

.PP
Internally, this function creates a temporary device and makes it available to you through \fBlibusb_get_device()\fP\&. This device is destroyed during \fBlibusb_close()\fP\&. The device shall not be opened through \fBlibusb_open()\fP\&.

.PP
This is a non-blocking function; no requests are sent over the bus\&.

.PP
Since version 1\&.0\&.23, \fBLIBUSB_API_VERSION\fP >= 0x01000107

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIctx\fP the context to operate on, or NULL for the default context 
.br
\fIsys_dev\fP the platform-specific system device handle 
.br
\fIdev_handle\fP output location for the returned device handle pointer\&. Only populated when the return code is 0\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 on success 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_NO_MEM\fP on memory allocation failure 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_ACCESS\fP if the user has insufficient permissions 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED\fP if the operation is not supported on this platform 

.PP
another LIBUSB_ERROR code on other failure 
.RE
.PP

